<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=solvay-process">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solvay process</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as ammonia–soda process</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Solvay process, or ammonia–soda process, is the main industrial method of making sodium carbonate (soda ash) from salt brine and limestone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkali">Alkali</a> had come from plant ashes, kelp and dry lakebeds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and from 1791 from the process of Nicolas Leblanc, whose expensive inputs and polluting by-products were drawbacks. The Belgian chemist Ernest Solvay turned to the problem in 1861 and, with a 24 m gas absorption tower and recycling of the ammonia, he and his brother Alfred built a plant at Couillet, near Charleroi, by 1864. By the 1890s Solvay plants produced most of the world's soda ash.</p></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>by 1864</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Couillet, Belgium</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Ernest Solvay · Alfred Solvay</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>It has been reported that Augustin Jean Fresnel found the central reaction in 1811 without publishing it, and a British patent of 1834 to H. G. Dyar and J. Hemming was an early attempt at an industrial process; Solvay was largely unaware of this earlier work.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=solvay-process">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
